1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.634
In this lesson, you'll learn several

2
00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:03.603
techniques to make your cache more accurate.

3
00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:08.475
like threshold tuning, cross-encoders, LLM checks, and fuzzy matching.

4
00:00:08.775 --> 00:00:10.777
Let's get coding. We'll use four

5
00:00:10.777 --> 00:00:13.079
different strategies to improve the cache quality.

6
00:00:13.146 --> 00:00:15.248
The first one is called threshold sweep.

7
00:00:15.248 --> 00:00:17.917
In this strategy, we evaluate the cache with different thresholds

8
00:00:17.917 --> 00:00:19.919
to find the one that maximizes the F1 Score.

9
00:00:19.919 --> 00:00:22.922
Looking at the graphics, we can see that increasing the threshold

10
00:00:22.922 --> 00:00:25.925
leads to better Recall, but it decreases the Precision.

11
00:00:25.925 --> 00:00:28.928
We see that F1, because it's a function of both of them,

12
00:00:28.928 --> 00:00:31.631
it maximizes at an intermediate threshold value.

13
00:00:31.631 --> 00:00:33.933
This is usually the threshold we want to pick.

14
00:00:33.933 --> 00:00:36.936
The second strategy is using a cross-encoder as a reranker.

15
00:00:36.936 --> 00:00:38.938
Cross-encoder is a model that can capture

16
00:00:38.938 --> 00:00:41.041
more nuances between the two sentences.

17
00:00:41.441 --> 00:00:43.943
because it processes them simultaneously.

18
00:00:44.244 --> 00:00:46.246
In this regard, it is more expressive,

19
00:00:46.346 --> 00:00:48.548
but it cannot be used in place of the embedding model

20
00:00:48.648 --> 00:00:52.052
because it does not produce vectors that are quick to search.

21
00:00:52.352 --> 00:00:55.555
In this specific example, we can see the Cross-Encoder Score

22
00:00:55.655 --> 00:00:57.557
and compare it to the embedding distance.

23
00:00:57.757 --> 00:01:00.360
As similarity increases, so does the Cross-Encoder Score,

24
00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:02.062
or its confidence in the similarity of the pair.

25
00:01:03.196 --> 00:01:05.698
The way we usually use the Cross Encoder effectively

26
00:01:05.698 --> 00:01:07.200
in the semantic caching system is

27
00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.935
by first retrieving a set of closest

28
00:01:08.935 --> 00:01:11.571
cache hits for a specific user query,

29
00:01:11.571 --> 00:01:13.306
and then running them through the Cross Encoder.

30
00:01:13.673 --> 00:01:16.876
We proceed by picking the cache hit with the highest similarity score.

31
00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:19.612
So in summary, we can expect a sizable boost in precision,

32
00:01:19.779 --> 00:01:22.482
especially if we fine-tune the model on our own data.

33
00:01:22.749 --> 00:01:24.651
But of course, this setup also

34
00:01:24.651 --> 00:01:26.853
increases the cache latency of our system.

35
00:01:27.153 --> 00:01:29.155
Our third strategy is to use an LLM

36
00:01:29.155 --> 00:01:32.559
and simply ask if a pair of sentences are semantically similar.

37
00:01:32.859 --> 00:01:34.327
To make this work effectively, we can

38
00:01:34.327 --> 00:01:36.062
use a cheaper LLM to do the validation.

39
00:01:36.062 --> 00:01:39.232
And also, validating similarity requires less tokens

40
00:01:39.232 --> 00:01:41.634
than generating a full user query response.

41
00:01:41.835 --> 00:01:43.837
and therefore is cheaper.

42
00:01:43.837 --> 00:01:46.339
In practice, you can also set it up as a fallback mechanism

43
00:01:46.339 --> 00:01:49.642
to query cache hits which have an ambiguous distance value.

44
00:01:49.843 --> 00:01:52.312
This validation schema can drastically increase precision,

45
00:01:52.312 --> 00:01:55.315
but it comes with increased latency and token usage

46
00:01:55.315 --> 00:01:57.717
compared to a system without an LLM validator.

47
00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:59.953
Lastly, let's look at fuzzy matching,

48
00:02:00.453 --> 00:02:02.856
an algorithmic strategy to matching strings.

49
00:02:03.156 --> 00:02:06.559
Fuzzy matching usually measures the edit distance between strings,

50
00:02:06.559 --> 00:02:10.063
or how quickly you can transform one string to the other

51
00:02:10.463 --> 00:02:13.266
using deletions, substitutions, and insertions.

52
00:02:13.566 --> 00:02:15.568
As you can see in the example on the slide,

53
00:02:15.568 --> 00:02:19.539
the two sentences with small difference produce a high similarity score.

54
00:02:19.739 --> 00:02:21.374
And the two sentences that are

55
00:02:21.374 --> 00:02:23.243
completely different have a very low score.

56
00:02:23.443 --> 00:02:25.245
This technique can be very helpful

57
00:02:25.445 --> 00:02:27.547
in keyword style search domains.

58
00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.682
where we can filter easily for typos.

59
00:02:29.983 --> 00:02:31.885
To set this up properly,

60
00:02:31.885 --> 00:02:33.386
we usually place the fuzzy matching

61
00:02:33.386 --> 00:02:35.088
in front of our semantic caching system,

62
00:02:35.088 --> 00:02:36.890
and with high similarity threshold,

63
00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:40.093
we can match typos to corresponding previous queries,

64
00:02:40.393 --> 00:02:42.695
short-circuiting the whole embedding generation

65
00:02:42.695 --> 00:02:44.797
and vector search mechanism after that.

66
00:02:45.098 --> 00:02:48.801
Setting this usually leads to decreased latency and increased precision.

67
00:02:49.102 --> 00:02:52.405
But it can lead to false positives if not set up correctly.

68
00:02:52.705 --> 00:02:54.507
Let's see all of these techniques in code.

69
00:02:54.908 --> 00:02:57.677
Let's start again by setting up our environment.

70
00:02:58.778 --> 00:03:00.780
and wait for this to load.

71
00:03:01.681 --> 00:03:05.084
And as previously, let's also again introduce the cache wrapper,

72
00:03:05.084 --> 00:03:07.387
which will help us hydrate and use our cache.

73
00:03:07.687 --> 00:03:10.089
Let's also instantiate our data container

74
00:03:10.089 --> 00:03:12.692
and get an instance of all our test queries.

75
00:03:13.159 --> 00:03:15.628
And just as a reminder from last time,

76
00:03:15.929 --> 00:03:17.931
we'll hydrate our cache,

77
00:03:18.131 --> 00:03:21.634
get all of the matches for a distance of 0.3,

78
00:03:21.935 --> 00:03:23.937
and run this through the evaluator.

79
00:03:24.137 --> 00:03:27.340
We'll obtain the previously seen evaluation report

80
00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:29.409
and use this as a baseline for

81
00:03:29.409 --> 00:03:31.444
the metrics that we're going to obtain further.

82
00:03:31.477 --> 00:03:33.613
Let's now look at our first

83
00:03:33.613 --> 00:03:36.082
improvement technique, which is the threshold sweep.

84
00:03:36.382 --> 00:03:39.886
We will start by checking all of the test queries

85
00:03:39.986 --> 00:03:43.089
that we have with a distance_threshold of one,

86
00:03:43.189 --> 00:03:45.191
obtaining a full retrieval.

87
00:03:45.391 --> 00:03:47.794
Then, we'll run the evaluator as previously,

88
00:03:47.894 --> 00:03:52.098
being mindful to construct it with a special constructor from_full_retrieval.

89
00:03:52.298 --> 00:03:55.368
Finally, let's look at this new helper function

90
00:03:55.468 --> 00:03:57.570
which is called report_threshold_sweep.

91
00:03:57.570 --> 00:04:00.940
The evaluator already has all the information necessary

92
00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:02.742
to run a threshold sweep for us

93
00:04:02.742 --> 00:04:05.845
and report the results in this nicely formatted report.

94
00:04:06.346 --> 00:04:08.548
As you can see on the diagram here,

95
00:04:09.048 --> 00:04:11.050
we have our precision going down

96
00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:14.153
with higher distance thresholds and recall going up.

97
00:04:14.554 --> 00:04:16.856
respectively. We see that the F1 maximizes

98
00:04:16.856 --> 00:04:19.359
in this specific value here,

99
00:04:19.359 --> 00:04:22.528
which if we look at the table with metrics,

100
00:04:22.528 --> 00:04:26.633
maximizes at threshold 0.2828,

101
00:04:26.633 --> 00:04:30.536
which gives us 90% precision and 85%

102
00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:33.172
recall at 25% cache hit rate,

103
00:04:33.473 --> 00:04:35.475
90% precision is pretty good,

104
00:04:35.575 --> 00:04:38.678
and we also have a decently sized cache hit rate.

105
00:04:39.178 --> 00:04:41.681
Looking at the confusion matrix, we can also see that

106
00:04:41.781 --> 00:04:43.249
most of our values lie on

107
00:04:43.249 --> 00:04:44.984
the diagonal, which is what we want.

108
00:04:44.984 --> 00:04:46.586
Now let's move on to the

109
00:04:46.586 --> 00:04:48.488
next technique that we're going to see.

110
00:04:48.788 --> 00:04:51.190
Running this code instantiates our cross encoder,

111
00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:53.493
which is based on Alibaba-NLP,

112
00:04:53.493 --> 00:04:57.797
which is a reranker based on modernbert-base.

113
00:04:57.997 --> 00:05:01.768
The cache wrapper also has another helper function called register_reranker,

114
00:05:01.768 --> 00:05:04.971
which when given a proper instance of a reranker,

115
00:05:04.971 --> 00:05:07.573
which can obtain from the cross encoder using

116
00:05:07.573 --> 00:05:10.176
this helper method. You could supply it here.

117
00:05:10.443 --> 00:05:14.647
and get a cache that has a reranking mechanism injected into it.

118
00:05:15.248 --> 00:05:18.151
But before that, let's look at an example of

119
00:05:18.151 --> 00:05:21.254
what the cross-encoder does, as we saw in the slides.

120
00:05:21.454 --> 00:05:23.222
The cross encoder is a model

121
00:05:23.222 --> 00:05:25.258
that can, given two pairs of sentences,

122
00:05:25.658 --> 00:05:27.827
give us a score of how similar

123
00:05:27.827 --> 00:05:29.962
they are or how relevant they are.

124
00:05:29.962 --> 00:05:31.597
In this case, we have What

125
00:05:31.597 --> 00:05:33.466
is the capital of China? versus Beijing,

126
00:05:33.466 --> 00:05:37.270
and we see that we have a very high similarity score.

127
00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:38.971
We also have sentence pairs of

128
00:05:38.971 --> 00:05:40.973
how to implement quick sort in python?

129
00:05:41.474 --> 00:05:44.377
which matches very well with Introduction of quick sort,

130
00:05:44.877 --> 00:05:46.512
and a bad match, which is again

131
00:05:46.512 --> 00:05:48.181
how to implement quick sort in python,

132
00:05:48.648 --> 00:05:51.351
matched against The weather is nice today,

133
00:05:51.651 --> 00:05:54.354
which is very dissimilar and has a very low score.

134
00:05:54.654 --> 00:05:57.957
Let's also see another example of a cross-encoder versus

135
00:05:58.458 --> 00:06:00.460
an embedding model.

136
00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:02.428
Here we're running the sentences both

137
00:06:02.428 --> 00:06:04.764
through the semantic cache and the cross-encoder.

138
00:06:05.264 --> 00:06:07.233
The sentences are as follows:

139
00:06:07.233 --> 00:06:09.435
The bank raised its interest rates.

140
00:06:09.435 --> 00:06:13.239
versus The river overflowed near the bank after heavy rain.

141
00:06:13.439 --> 00:06:16.209
They are intentionally confusing. And we can see

142
00:06:16.209 --> 00:06:18.945
that even though they are relatively far apart,

143
00:06:20.246 --> 00:06:23.516
the cross-encoder, rightfully, puts them very far apart,

144
00:06:23.916 --> 00:06:27.320
while the embedding model is relatively confused.

145
00:06:28.087 --> 00:06:30.890
It is also important to note that here a cross encoder

146
00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:34.894
distance is constructed by doing one minus the cross encoder score

147
00:06:34.894 --> 00:06:36.996
that we saw previously here.

148
00:06:37.296 --> 00:06:39.298
Okay. So let's now obtain results

149
00:06:39.298 --> 00:06:42.602
from the cross encoder and run it to our evaluator.

150
00:06:43.002 --> 00:06:46.305
We're running again now our cache wrapper

151
00:06:46.305 --> 00:06:48.474
in which we register the cross encoder.

152
00:06:48.474 --> 00:06:50.476
We're running it with the test queries.

153
00:06:50.676 --> 00:06:52.678
using a distance_threshold of one,

154
00:06:52.678 --> 00:06:55.081
which means that we're doing a full retrieval here.

155
00:06:55.081 --> 00:06:56.883
We're using num_results 10,

156
00:06:56.883 --> 00:06:59.852
which means that the cross-encoder is going to

157
00:06:59.852 --> 00:07:03.189
look into and evaluate 10 examples and rerank them.

158
00:07:03.189 --> 00:07:06.659
And again, we are instantiating the evaluator

159
00:07:06.659 --> 00:07:10.463
using the special constructor with the from_full_retrieval.

160
00:07:10.463 --> 00:07:14.267
Let's look at the results from the threshold sweep report.

161
00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:17.203
Interestingly, now we can obtain a much higher precision

162
00:07:17.904 --> 00:07:19.806
at 94%

163
00:07:20.406 --> 00:07:23.509
with relatively decent recall, 79%,

164
00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:26.712
and still good cache hit rate of 23%.

165
00:07:26.913 --> 00:07:30.516
And this we obtained at a threshold of 0.11.

166
00:07:31.017 --> 00:07:34.520
Moving on, let's now look at the system

167
00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:37.623
using an LLM validator in our cache.

168
00:07:38.124 --> 00:07:41.527
Because this is using an LLM, let's load our OpenAI key.

169
00:07:41.828 --> 00:07:44.630
And let's instantiate our LLMEvaluator.

170
00:07:45.231 --> 00:07:48.034
We can instantiate it similarly in the cache_wrapper

171
00:07:48.034 --> 00:07:52.038
by constructing it and passing it to the register_reranker method.

172
00:07:54.040 --> 00:07:57.043
We continue by running the cache_wrapper.

173
00:07:57.410 --> 00:07:59.512
that has the LLMEvaluator

174
00:07:59.612 --> 00:08:04.016
with a threshold that we already know it's relatively good.

175
00:08:08.621 --> 00:08:13.025
So this will potentially improve our results even further

176
00:08:13.125 --> 00:08:16.229
by filtering out false positive results.

177
00:08:17.063 --> 00:08:20.533
Let's run the cache results through the evaluator.

178
00:08:21.033 --> 00:08:23.970
As you can see, we obtained 100% precision.

179
00:08:24.470 --> 00:08:27.540
This means that we removed all of our false positives.

180
00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:30.176
Our recall dropped significantly,

181
00:08:30.476 --> 00:08:32.678
but we still have a relatively

182
00:08:32.979 --> 00:08:34.780
high cache hit rate.

183
00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:38.985
Now, let's run the semantic cache with the LLM validator

184
00:08:38.985 --> 00:08:41.587
through the evaluation that we've seen previously.

185
00:08:41.587 --> 00:08:43.289
We have 100% precision,

186
00:08:43.289 --> 00:08:44.991
which means that we filtered out

187
00:08:44.991 --> 00:08:46.993
all of our false positives.

188
00:08:47.426 --> 00:08:50.229
but we also acquired a lot of false negatives,

189
00:08:50.229 --> 00:08:52.632
so things that could have hit but did not because

190
00:08:52.632 --> 00:08:54.233
the LLM was too strict.

191
00:08:54.634 --> 00:08:58.437
This might be still valuable in a lot of domains,

192
00:08:58.838 --> 00:09:01.340
and we achieve a relatively high cache hit rate

193
00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:02.942
of 21%, while

194
00:09:03.509 --> 00:09:05.711
having 100% precision.

195
00:09:06.312 --> 00:09:09.315
Lastly, we arrived at the last section of our lesson.

196
00:09:09.315 --> 00:09:11.517
We're going to explore Fuzzy Matching.

197
00:09:11.517 --> 00:09:14.820
We'll start by introducing a simple function called fuzzify_string.

198
00:09:15.388 --> 00:09:19.091
Basically gets a string as input, a number of iterations

199
00:09:19.292 --> 00:09:22.795
where it introduces a random swaps within strings.

200
00:09:23.396 --> 00:09:26.098
So because Fuzzy Matching is a useful technique

201
00:09:26.098 --> 00:09:28.801
to use in keyword style searches,

202
00:09:28.901 --> 00:09:31.270
where the queries and the cache hits

203
00:09:31.370 --> 00:09:34.173
are relatively similar syntactically.

204
00:09:34.840 --> 00:09:36.842
We're going to augment our data set

205
00:09:37.343 --> 00:09:41.347
to make it more appropriate for evaluation in this specific setting.

206
00:09:41.747 --> 00:09:44.750
So we're going to construct a special data set.

207
00:09:45.017 --> 00:09:47.420
to validate and showcase this technique,

208
00:09:47.420 --> 00:09:50.823
where we're going to use the FAQ questions

209
00:09:50.823 --> 00:09:53.225
and we're going to generate pairs

210
00:09:53.225 --> 00:09:56.829
with varying degree of difficulty or fuzziness,

211
00:09:56.829 --> 00:09:59.031
which is introduced with this function,

212
00:09:59.031 --> 00:10:02.501
which does random swaps between neighboring characters.

213
00:10:02.501 --> 00:10:06.405
Let's take a look at the fuzzified queries that we've generated here.

214
00:10:06.439 --> 00:10:09.642
these queries at a varying degree of fuzziness.

215
00:10:09.942 --> 00:10:11.844
We start by a fuzziness of

216
00:10:11.844 --> 00:10:14.046
two, which does only two random swaps.

217
00:10:14.246 --> 00:10:18.451
Then we go to three, four, 10, and then a hundred

218
00:10:18.451 --> 00:10:21.654
where the string looks almost unrecognizable.

219
00:10:21.954 --> 00:10:25.458
After that, we're going to instantiate an abstraction called FuzzyCache.

220
00:10:26.092 --> 00:10:29.462
which has a very similar interface as the semantic cache wrapper

221
00:10:29.462 --> 00:10:31.364
that we have worked with so far.

222
00:10:31.764 --> 00:10:34.967
We can hydrate it using our FAQ data set.

223
00:10:35.368 --> 00:10:37.370
We can check multiple queries.

224
00:10:41.007 --> 00:10:43.209
Looking at the fuzzy query retrievals,

225
00:10:43.209 --> 00:10:46.412
we have again the query and the matches that it gets.

226
00:10:46.912 --> 00:10:49.415
As you can see for complex cases like these ones,

227
00:10:49.715 --> 00:10:53.719
they get matched to their corresponding queries rightfully.

228
00:10:54.286 --> 00:10:57.690
And even for this really difficult example,

229
00:10:57.790 --> 00:11:01.494
we still managed to map it to the correct cache entry.

230
00:11:02.094 --> 00:11:05.097
We'll continue by generating a valid fuzzy labeling,

231
00:11:05.798 --> 00:11:08.000
which basically captures all of the pairs

232
00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:11.103
that are valid query and cache hit matches.

233
00:11:11.904 --> 00:11:13.906
We'll use this to label

234
00:11:14.073 --> 00:11:16.175
our retrievals,

235
00:11:16.375 --> 00:11:18.277
and because we have this labeling,

236
00:11:18.377 --> 00:11:20.579
we can use it in the CacheEvaluator,

237
00:11:20.680 --> 00:11:24.283
where we're going to pass it here in the true_labels argument.

238
00:11:25.117 --> 00:11:29.155
and we're going to report the metrics for a specific distance threshold,

239
00:11:29.455 --> 00:11:32.725
where these retrievals, which in this case is 0.6.

240
00:11:32.992 --> 00:11:37.263
and we obtained 100% precision, 94% recall.

241
00:11:37.897 --> 00:11:40.099
and have a very high cache hit rate.

242
00:11:40.366 --> 00:11:43.069
But this example is very contrived in a way

243
00:11:43.369 --> 00:11:46.672
and rather small scale, and just serves to showcase

244
00:11:46.672 --> 00:11:48.340
that you can actually set up and use

245
00:11:48.340 --> 00:11:49.975
such a system in front of your cache.

246
00:11:50.376 --> 00:11:52.378
Let's also clean up our cache here.

247
00:11:52.678 --> 00:11:56.082
In the next lesson, you're going to implement your first AI agent

